+# cluster ci-8764ffa3b495466c - hosted Qt 5 build stopped by a Qt 6-only QStringList constructor
+
+FIXED
+
+## Verdict and evidence
+
+REAL. At `fa4a5f2e785c73e32216931e33cdbe140c06647d` the hosted jobs build against Qt 5.15,
+which stopped at `tests/src/gui/MixerSendsOnFadersTest.cpp:278`:
+
+```cpp
+QVERIFY(faders == QStringList(channels, QStringLiteral("volume")));
+```
+
+`QStringList` in Qt 6 inherits `QList<QString>`'s constructors, including
+`QList(qsizetype size, parameter_type t)`; Qt 5.15's `QStringList` does not inherit that
+constructor (its only two-argument candidate is the iterator range
+`QStringList(InputIterator first, InputIterator last)`), so deduction fails because
+`channels` is `int` and the second argument is a `QString`. The hosted diagnostics are the
+same on every failed job:
+
+- `/home/kruzzzzy/.local/share/zene-bunny-fix/qwen/train-ci/fa4a5f2e785c73e32216931e33cdbe140c06647d/run-43786984969-job-133036727687.log:9084`
+  (`windows-arm64`): `error: no matching constructor for initialization of 'QStringList'`,
+  candidate template ignored because `InputIterator` deduced `int` vs `QString`.
+- the same line and error in `run-43786984969-job-133036727623.log` (`linux-arm64`),
+  `run-43786984969-job-133036727661.log` (`linux-x86_64`),
+  `run-43786984969-job-133036727658.log` (`macos-x86_64`) and
+  `run-43786984969-job-133036727772.log` (`macos-arm64`).
+- Those five logs contain exactly this compile error as the cause of the failed build (the
+  Windows log's other `error:` line is the package manager's "failed to reopen stdin" warning,
+  not a source error).
+
+The local build tree is Qt 6.12, where the line compiles, which is why the checkout alone did
+not reproduce it; the new regression compiles the real source statement against the installed
+Qt 5.15 tree and fails with the hosted diagnostic (see RED below).
+
+## Fix at the cause
+
+`tests/src/gui/MixerSendsOnFadersTest.cpp:278-280` now builds the same all-volume list with a
+Qt 5/6 portable append loop:
+
+```cpp
+QStringList expected;
+for (int index = 0; index < channels; ++index) { expected.append(QStringLiteral("volume")); }
+QVERIFY(faders == expected);
+```
+
+The assertion, its size, and the comparison are unchanged: `channels` copies of "volume".
+A repository-wide search found no other `QStringList(<identifier>, ...)` or
+`QList<QString>(<identifier>, ...)` construction:
+
+```sh
+grep -rnE "QStringList\s*\(\s*[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*\s*," tests src include plugins --include=*.cpp --include=*.h
+grep -rnE "QList<\s*QString\s*>\s*\(\s*[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*\s*," tests src include plugins --include=*.cpp --include=*.h
+```
+
+both exited 1 with no matches.
+
+## Regression
+
+New source-derived Qt 5 compiler check: `tests/Qt5QStringListSizeConstructorCheck.py`
+(registered as CTest `Qt5QStringListSizeConstructorCheck` at `tests/CMakeLists.txt:6272-6283`).
+
+- `derived_statement()` (line 78) reads the actual `QVERIFY(faders == ...)` statement and the
+  expected-list construction immediately above it in `MixerSendsOnFadersTest.cpp`.
+- the derived statement is compiled `-fsyntax-only -std=gnu++20` against `pkg-config Qt5Test`
+  (the hosted compiler input), and against Qt 6 when that tree is installed.
+- the failing control is the Qt 6-only `QStringList(channels, QStringLiteral("volume"))` shape
+  (line 48); the check reports itself skipped, never passed, if the Qt 5 tree accepts it.
+- `--self-test` requires the same tree to reject the Qt 6-only shape and accept the
+  append-built list.
